Task 1: Set up Vault to Manage Secrets Across AWS, Azure, and GCP
Subtask 1.1: Install HashiCorp Vault
First, we'll install Vault on our Linux machine and set up the basic configuration.

Step 1: Update the system and install required packages

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y wget unzip curl jq
Step 2: Download and install Vault

# Download the latest Vault binary
wget https://releases.hashicorp.com/vault/1.15.2/vault_1.15.2_linux_amd64.zip

# Unzip the binary
unzip vault_1.15.2_linux_amd64.zip

# Move to system path
sudo mv vault /usr/local/bin/

# Verify installation
vault version
Step 3: Create Vault configuration directory and files

# Create Vault directories
sudo mkdir -p /etc/vault.d
sudo mkdir -p /opt/vault/data

# Create Vault configuration file
sudo tee /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl > /dev/null <<EOF
ui = true
disable_mlock = true

storage "file" {
  path = "/opt/vault/data"
}

listener "tcp" {
  address     = "0.0.0.0:8200"
  tls_disable = 1
}

api_addr = "http://127.0.0.1:8200"
cluster_addr = "https://127.0.0.1:8201"
EOF
Subtask 1.2: Initialize and Configure Vault
Step 1: Start Vault server

# Start Vault in development mode for this lab
vault server -config=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl &

# Wait for Vault to start
sleep 5

# Set environment variable
export VAULT_ADDR='http://127.0.0.1:8200'
Step 2: Initialize Vault

# Initialize Vault
vault operator init -key-shares=1 -key-threshold=1 > vault-init.txt

# Extract unseal key and root token
UNSEAL_KEY=$(grep 'Unseal Key 1:' vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')
ROOT_TOKEN=$(grep 'Initial Root Token:' vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')

# Unseal Vault
vault operator unseal $UNSEAL_KEY

# Login with root token
vault auth $ROOT_TOKEN
Subtask 1.3: Enable Secret Engines for Multi-cloud
Step 1: Enable AWS secrets engine

# Enable AWS secrets engine
vault secrets enable -path=aws aws

# Configure AWS secrets engine (using mock credentials for lab)
